Z||000000||montage||02-08-2001||09:45 AM||||This is a hypothetical question about axial ratio and the effect on performance. I would like to know from the members if you had your choice what would be the ideal axial ratio to increase the performance of an 88 formula 5-sp (stock so far but runs strong) in 0-60, ¼ mile, and possibly autocross. I've done searches on the forum without much luck on what axial ratio does to performance maybe those with 4.10 ratio could comment on the benefits. Maybe you guys (no offense ladies generic term I'm a Yooper) with desktop dynos could calculate the effect of axial ratio for 3.6, 3.7, 3.8, 3.9, 4, and 4.1 (maybe others?). I am not concerned with top speed and this is the first part of a two-part question that I will post after getting your comments. Thanks a bunch!||||reg||1
Z||000001||montage||02-08-2001||09:50 AM||||Opps sorry about the spelling error I mean axle!! Dangers of spell checkers!||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-08-2001||07:54 PM||||First, you need the actual fianl ratio's available for the transmission in question. There's no sense running numbers for gears you can't get.<P>Second, "effective" ratio and tranny final drive ratio aren't always the same. Effective ratio is the output you get when you take the final gears and mate them to a particular set of tires. You need to know the rolling diameter of the tires for this part of the math. (No I don't have the formula for it. Someone posted a link to a really nice calculator that told you some or all this part. Do a search in general. It was there I think. It's been quite qwhile since I've seen it.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||02-08-2001||08:30 PM||||With a stock 2.8-liter fiero weighing 2925 lbs. with the driver and the Getrag 5-speed transmission it will go 0-60 mph in 7.964 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.72 seconds @ 86 mph. If you install a 4.10 final drive ratio you will get better off the line and low speed execration but loose a little top end speed in return. With the 4.10 final drive ratio that same fiero will go 0-60 mph in 7.79 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.73 seconds @ 87 mph. Your 60-foot time with the 3.61 final drive would be 2.29 seconds and 2.26 seconds with the 4.10 final drive ratio.<P>I think you can only change the final drive ratio to a 4.10 on the four-speed transmission. The H.O. quad four engine with the Getrag transmission had a lower final drive ratio so maybe you could use that transmission with one of Rodney Dickmans conversion kits or just pirate the final drive. I don’t know what the ratio is exactly but it something like 3.90.  <P>Does this answer your question? I got these figures using Dragstrip2000. <P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||montage||02-09-2001||06:21 AM||||Thanks for the info on the times 88. I see your point theogre but I need new tires for the summer and my thought is to go with a smaller diameter tire to do several things, first change the "effective" gear ratio (tires looking at give an effective 3.83 ratio) and lower the car 0.7 inches retaining the stock travel geometry. Since tires have a large affect on handling I think of this as a low cost way to lower the car and change effective gear ratio. That is my interest in these calculations to see if other ratios have an advantage. My last note is my main priority is performance/cost not looks. Any comments on this idea would be appreciated.||||reg||1
